  "learningBlocks": [
    {
      "id": "l8-b1",
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Two more cases become functional",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The genitive and dative have been visible since Lesson 2. Now they become active reading tools. Genitive commonly marks relationship or possession; dative commonly marks recipient or reference.",
        "fr": "Le génitif et le datif sont visibles depuis la leçon 2. Ils deviennent maintenant des outils actifs de lecture. Le génitif marque souvent la relation ou la possession ; le datif, le destinataire ou la référence."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8-b2",
      "type": "case-panel",
      "title": "Genitive: of whom? of what?",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "In Agnus Dei, Dei means 'of God'. In peccata mundi, mundi means 'of the world'. Read the relationship before trying to translate every word.",
        "fr": "Dans Agnus Dei, Dei signifie « de Dieu ». Dans peccata mundi, mundi signifie « du monde ». Lisez d'abord la relation avant de traduire chaque mot."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8-b3",
      "type": "case-panel",
      "title": "Dative: to whom? for whom?",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "In Deo gratias, Deo is the dative recipient/reference: thanks to God. The same pattern appears in Patri, Filio and Spiritui Sancto.",
        "fr": "Dans Deo gratias, Deo est le datif du destinataire ou de la référence : grâce à Dieu. Le même schéma apparaît dans Patri, Filio et Spiritui Sancto."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8-b4",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Agnus Dei",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-agnus-dei-l8",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Agnus is the head noun; Dei tells whose Lamb, and mundi tells whose sins. One short formula already contains two genitive relationships.",
        "fr": "Agnus est le nom principal ; Dei indique de qui est l'Agneau, et mundi à quel monde appartiennent les péchés. Une seule formule contient déjà deux relations au génitif."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-agnus-dei-l8",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8-b5",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Deo gratias",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-deo-gratias-l8",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "The fixed response is useful precisely because it is so short: Deo cannot be read as a new vocabulary word. It is Deus in the dative.",
        "fr": "Cette réponse fixe est utile parce qu'elle est très courte : Deo n'est pas un nouveau mot de vocabulaire, mais Deus au datif."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-deo-gratias-l8",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l8-b6",
      "type": "liturgical-reading",
      "title": "Gloria Patri",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-gloria-patri-l8",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Patri, Filio and Spiritui are parallel datives. Once you see the case relationship, the sentence becomes structurally transparent even before every declension is automatic.",
        "fr": "Patri, Filio et Spiritui sont des datifs parallèles. Une fois la relation de cas reconnue, la structure devient claire avant même que toutes les déclinaisons soient automatiques."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-gloria-patri-l8",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    }
  ],
